{"id": "section-body-04853", "title": "Management of Malaria in Special Groups", "chapterId": "chapter-five", "blocks": [{"id": "body-04853", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading2", "text": "Management of Malaria in Special Groups", "runs": [{"text": "Management"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Malaria"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Special"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Groups"}], "level": 2, "list": {"level": 1, "numberingId": "1748"}}, {"id": "body-04855", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Malaria in Pregnancy", "runs": [{"text": "Malaria"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Pregnancy"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "1748"}}, {"id": "body-04856", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "The effects of malaria in pregnancy are related to the malaria endemicity, with abortion more common in areas of low endemicity and intrauterine growth retardation more common in areas of high endemicity.", "runs": [{"text": "The"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "effects"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "malaria"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pregnancy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "related"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "malaria"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "endemicity,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "abortion"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "more"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "common"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "areas"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "low endemicity and intrauterine growth retardation more common in areas of high endemicity."}]}, {"id": "body-04857", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Uncomplicated Malaria in Pregnancy", "runs": [{"text": "Uncomplicated"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Malaria"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Pregnancy"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1748"}}, {"id": "body-04858", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "In high-transmission areas (moderate to high immunity); malaria is usually asymptomatic in pregnancy or is associated with only mild, non-specific symptoms. (See section 5.1 above)", "runs": [{"text": "In high-transmission areas (moderate to high immunity); malaria is usually asymptomatic in pregnancy or is associated with only mild, non-specific symptoms. (See section 5.1 above)"}]}, {"id": "body-04860", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Pharmacological Management", "runs": [{"text": "Pharmacological"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Management"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-04861", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Artemether/Lumefantrine (ALU) is the recommended treatment of choice of a confirmed uncomplicated malaria to pregnant women in all trimesters. See table 5.1 above", "runs": [{"text": "Artemether/Lumefantrine (ALU) is the recommended treatment of choice of a confirmed uncomplicated malaria to pregnant women in all trimesters. See table 5.1 above"}]}, {"id": "body-04862", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Severe Malaria in Pregnancy", "runs": [{"text": "Severe"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Malaria"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Pregnancy"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1748"}}, {"id": "body-04863", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "In low-transmission areas (low malaria immunity); women in the second and third trimesters of pregnancy are more likely to develop severe malaria than other adults, often complicated by pulmonary oedema and hypoglycaemia.", "runs": [{"text": "In low-transmission areas (low malaria immunity); women in the second and third trimesters of pregnancy are more likely to develop severe malaria than other adults, often complicated by pulmonary oedema and hypoglycaemia."}]},